In this lesson, we'll look at how to test, refine, and evaluate a geometric algorithm once you've written it down. Think of it like this: your algorithm isn't really finished until it works for every case it's supposed to handle. So the first step is simple. Just run it on a representative example, then check the output against your goal, whether that's a perpendicular line, a midpoint, or equal angles. If it fails, don't panic. Usually it just means one step was a bit vague. Go back and tighten it up. Next, push your algorithm with edge cases. Try a very small angle, a point that sits right on the line, two sides that happen to be equal, or really long segments. Each test should expose a new situation you hadn't thought of. When you find something that breaks, refine the wording. Maybe you need to specify a radius instead of just saying "draw a circle", or add a quick check for when two points coincide. You can also combine steps that always go together. Finally, step back and evaluate. Does it always finish? Does it only use your allowed tools? And is the output actually unique? Now let's see it in action.